            <ArticleTitle>Unexpected uptake of gadoxetic acid in a hepatic metastasis from T-cell lymphoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>122-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1102/1470-7330.2012.0020</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of unexpected heterogeneous uptake of gadoxetic acid into a hepatic metastasis in a patient with T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma that also lacked hypermetabolic characteristics on positron emission (PET)/computed tomography (CT) with [(18)F]fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG). Therefore, in cases of heterogeneous uptake of gadoxetic acid, infiltrative lesions must be considered.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
